{"text":"With a=σ(g), additive fusion h+a·r preserves coefficient 1 on h; it cannot directly gate away stale information already in h, although r may learn cancellation. Convex fusion (1−a)h+a·r=h+a(r−h) explicitly attenuates h and can approach replacement by recent interests. This is an algebraic distinction, not a demonstrated accuracy advantage. [SLi-Rec’s implementation](https://github.com/Ethan-Lyc/SeqRec/blob/master/sli_rec.py) uses complementary sigmoid weights, conditioning its gate on long/short representations, target embedding and elapsed time. [AdaMCT](https://arxiv.org/html/2205.08776v3) provides adaptive local/global mixture ablations and LayerNorm in both branches, but neither establishes a controlled comparison of these exact two formulas.

Normalization matters because unequal branch magnitudes can dominate nominal gate weights. [SASRec’s PyTorch implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/pmixer/SASRec.pytorch/master/python/model.py) scales input embeddings by √d and applies final LayerNorm before dot-product scoring. A reproducible proposed comparison: separately normalize h and projected r to the same dimension, then hold output normalization and scoring constant across fusion variants. Gate initialization remains an experimental choice: zero gate weights plus bias 0 gives a=0.5; bias −2 gives a≈0.119 and initially favors h. These are suggested controls, not verified paper defaults. With scalar gates, post-fusion normalization can obscure scale differences between additive and convex variants.

For causal recent-window pooling, a proposed implementation is r_t=Σ_j m_tj·e_j/max(1,Σ_j m_tj), with m_tj=1[item_j≠0]·1[t−w+1≤j≤t]; zero padded query outputs afterward. This excludes padding and future targets at every training position. Pooling contextual states can retain older information through their receptive fields; raw item embeddings provide stricter locality. No located source jointly ablated this pooling rule, gate initialization, normalization and stale-interest suppression; validate those claims separately."}
